<!--  demoted to supporting asides after the two lead stories.          -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-cleaning-recoding" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Cleaning and Recoding Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-cleaning-recoding">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-exploring-data" /> quietly skipped over something. When
      <c>mean(flamingos$body_mass_g)</c> returned <c>NA</c>, we set the
      missing values aside with <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> and moved on, promising
      to come back to it properly. This chapter is that promise kept, and
      more besides.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Cleaning</term> is the work of finding what is wrong, missing or
      inconsistent in a dataset before you trust it. <term>Recoding</term> is
      the work of transforming variables, categories, and codes into a form
      that actually answers your question. Both sound like housekeeping
      <mdash /> something to clear out of the way before the real analysis
      starts. They are not. The decisions you make while cleaning and
      recoding decide what a missing value is allowed to mean, which records
      count, and which get quietly set aside <mdash /> which makes them part
      of the analysis, not preparation for it. Skip them, rush them, or hand
      them to software without supervision, and you can get a confident,
      precisely wrong answer out the other end. That happened, at the
      largest possible scale, during the deadliest outbreak of Ebola virus
      disease in history.
    </p>
  </introduction>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 1: Ebola, West Africa 2014-2016                          -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-ebola-data">
    <title>A Preventable Disaster</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        From 2014 to 2016, an outbreak of Ebola virus disease swept
        through Guinea, Liberia, and Sierra Leone. There were more than
        11,000 reported deaths — the largest Ebola epidemic ever
        recorded.
      </p>

      <p>
        Fighting an epidemic this size depends on knowing, as close to
        real time as possible, where cases are appearing, how fast they are
        growing, and who each infected person has been in contact with. All
        of that depends on data. Two independent accounts, written by
        people who worked inside the response, describe how that
        data was managed at both the national level and in individual
        treatment centres. One of these accounts is very explicit about the
        consequence: the state of the data management system <q>may have 
        contributed to the magnitude and long duration of the outbreak.</q>
        This is a story about how failing to clean and reconcile data through
        duplicate records, inconsistent values, and incomplete data
        may have helped an epidemic run longer and grow larger, resulting in
        over 11,000 deaths.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-two-systems">
      <title>Two systems, neither reliable on its own</title>
      <p>
        Owada et al. (2016) describe a national data management system
        built from two parallel tools that were never properly reconciled
        with each other: The Epi Info VHF line list database, and an Excel-based
        line list.
      </p>

      <p>
        Every case was meant to be recorded on a paper "case
        investigation form" (CIF). This was to be filled in in the field,
        and then subsequently entered into the <term>Epi Info VHF line
        list database</term>, a tool built by the US Centers for Disease
        Control specifically for outbreak case and contact-tracing data.
        In principle, it supported multiple people entering data
        at once and merging district databases into a national one.
        In practice, most district data managers were not
        experienced users of the software, duplicate records were
        genuinely difficult to detect and merge once created, and CIFs
        routinely arrived late at the district response centres. This meant
        the VHF database was never fully up to date, even late in the
        outbreak.
      </p>

      <p>
        To add, laboratory results and case investigation data were often
        entered <em>independently</em> of each other instead of the lab
        result being added to the existing associated case record.
        This meant that the same case could end up as two separate database
        entries, with no link to combine them together.
      </p>

      <p>
        As well as the Epi Info database, district teams also kept a second,
        entirely separate
        <term>Excel-based line list</term>. This was built mainly from laboratory
        sample results and contained a subset of the CIF variables like age,
        sex, location, and the dates a sample was collected, received, and
        tested. The district teams found that this was quicker to produce
        reports and visualise the situation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-collection">
      <title>Incomplete at the source</title>
      <p>
        Even when the systems worked as designed, the data was
        often incomplete. Many CIF records had no date of onset, sex,
        age, and/or residence. These are all gaps that the authors
        attribute to inadequate training and supervision of surveillance
        officers under outbreak conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        In addition, the number of deaths from EVD were hard to pin down.
        Confirming a death was
        EVD-related required a post-mortem mouth swab, which was
        often not taken in areas that
        continued traditional burial practices. This meant that the count of
        <em>confirmed</em> EVD deaths likely
        understated the true number. In the other direction, before the outbreak, Sierra
        Leone was already dealing with malaria severe enough
        to account for roughly 38% of hospital admissions and about 30% of
        under-five deaths. Importantly, malaria shares clinical features
        with EVD. That overlap plausibly delayed recognition of genuine EVD
        cases, and risked malaria deaths being misclassified as EVD deaths.
        The same underlying data problem of incomplete, hard-to-verify
        records pushed the death count in both directions at once,
        for different reasons, in different places.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-transmission">
      <title>Lost between the form and the file</title>

      <p>
        The intended process was that two copies of the
        original CIF would be produced for every death, and
        three copies for every suspected living case:
        one to accompany the sample to the laboratory, one to the
        district response centre, and for suspected cases, one to stay with
        the patient. In the early period of the outbreak this process did
        not function effectively. Reporting of deaths from holding centres,
        community care centres and treatment centres had no clear, structured
        process until daily phone calls were later introduced, and
        burial teams, treatment centres, and laboratories used inconsistent
        definitions when counting deaths in the first place. A meaningful
        amount of information was simply never transmitted anywhere it could
        be used.
      </p>

      <p>
        What did arrive still had to be compiled, and there was no
        consolidated database that captured and linked every data source in
        a structured way. Each district response centre had adopted its own
        method for recording cases, and inconsistency in the Excel line-list
        format across districts caused ongoing problems merging and
        aggregating cases into national totals. In the authors' own words,
        <q>much of the data has never found its way into any structured
        database and may never be recoverable.</q>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-cfr">
      <title>A number that was biased whichever way you calculated it</title>

      <p>
        The clearest illustration of what this cost is
        the <term>case fatality rate</term> (CFR). CFR is the confirmed
        deaths divided
        by confirmed cases. This basic number in an outbreak helps
        track both how dangerous the disease is, and how contained it is.
      </p>

      <p>
        Undercounting deaths means that the CFR calculation was biased downward,
        and the severity of the situation is underestimated.
        Further, if you restrict the calculation to cases with a known final
        outcome (alive or dead), then a new bias is introduced:
        <q>alive</q> could only be recorded once a patient's hospitalisation
        had fully concluded, while a death could be recorded as soon as it
        happened. Cases still awaiting an outcome were therefore more likely,
        at any given moment, to eventually resolve as deaths than the data
        so far suggested, which biases <em>this</em> version of the CFR
        upward instead. Neither calculation was wrong in the sense of
        containing an arithmetic error, but both were systematically biased, in
        opposite directions, by exactly the same underlying data problems.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-ebola-cfr-lesson">
        <title>No clean number was hiding underneath</title>
        <p>
          There was no clean version of this number sitting underneath the
          messy data. The data management failures described above added noise
          around a true value, but also changed which direction the bias in the
          headline statistic ran.
          The system meant to hold the full picture could
          not be trusted to give one, so analysts instead used laboratory data
          for day-to-day situation reports for the rest of the outbreak.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        The authors are explicit that this was never fully resolved: a
        complete cleaning and reconciliation of all case data was never
        finalised.
        Even at the end of the outbreak, a large number of suspected
        cases remained permanently unclassified - neither confirmed
        as EVD nor ruled out.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-clinical">
      <title>The same failure, one level down</title>

      <p>
        Roshania et al. (2016) tell a parallel story from inside the
        treatment centres themselves. International Medical Corps ran five
        Ebola Treatment Units across Liberia and Sierra Leone, treating more
        than 2,500 patients and amassing over 25,000 pages of clinical,
        epidemiological, and operational data over the course of the
        epidemic.
      </p>

      <p>
        Even a single, well-resourced organisation running its own units
        under its own protocols could not keep that data consistent.

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Different treatment units recorded different variables in
            different formats. For example, units used Microsoft Access,
            other units used Excel. This data had to be reconciled before
            it could be analysed together.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Data-entry was not consistent. It varied between
            treatment units and, within a treatment unit.
            This made it difficult to track the severity of the epidemic.
            More patients meant more
            data to enter, and staff availability did not scale up to
            match. The moment data quality was most likely to slip was
            at the peak of the outbreak <mdash /> exactly the moment
            when accurate data mattered most.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Paper forms were filled out inside the high-risk treatment zone.
            However, these forms physically degraded from repeated chlorine
            disinfection. Getting
            the data out safely meant reading forms through a fence and
            copying them by hand, photographing them across a barrier, or
            sending someone in full protective equipment purely to
            photograph paperwork with a waterproof camera.
            Every additional step introduced opportunities for transcription
            errors, typos, missing values, or misread information. 
            This gives an additional challenge of ensuring that the
            data remained accurate as it moved from the original form into
            a usable dataset. Small errors introduced during the
            transfer could easily become part of the analysis and ultimately
            affect the decisions made from the data.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Some variables, such as recent bush meat consumption or travel
            history, were recorded in some forms and not others,
            making a combined dataset impossible without deciding
            what to do about the gap.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The same clinical sign was written down under different names in
            different places (<q>red injected eyes,</q>
            <q>conjunctivitis,</q> <q>hemorrhagic eyes</q>) and had
            to be manually recoded into a single consistent variable before
            it meant anything as data.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        An initial quality audit <mdash /> a random sample of patient records
        compared field by field against the original scanned charts <mdash />
        found error rates high enough that the organisation had to re-enter
        large sections of the dataset by hand. Even then, resources were
        limited enough that a choice had to be made about what to re-enter:
        triage data was redone for every patient, but the more detailed
        daily rounding and treatment data was only redone for patients
        confirmed EVD-positive. After that effort, they reached roughly 99%
        accuracy. Getting there took a dedicated data-cleaning team working
        <em>after the fact</em>, months later, on data collected under some
        of the most dangerous conditions imaginable.
      </p>

      <p>
        The organisation's own retrospective judgement is blunt: <q>much
        time and effort could have been saved through closer oversight and
        real-time quality assurance assessments in the field.</q> The
        cleaning was not the problem. Doing all of it afterward, once the
        epidemic had already moved on, was.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-ebola-lesson">
        <title>Cleaning was not optional overhead here</title>
        <p>
          In both accounts, the failure to standardise, deduplicate, and
          consolidate case data was not a data-management inconvenience
          sitting alongside the real work of fighting the epidemic. It
          <em>was</em> the epidemic response, failing at one of its most
          basic tasks: knowing, accurately, what was happening. When you
          cannot calculate a case fatality rate or reliably merge two
          districts' records, you cannot see the outbreak clearly enough to
          fight it well.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Owada, K., Eckmanns, T., Kamara, K.O.B. and
        Olu, O.O. (2016). <q>Epidemiological Data Management during an
        Outbreak of Ebola Virus Disease: Key Issues and Observations from
        Sierra Leone.</q> <em>Frontiers in Public Health</em>, 4, 163.
        Roshania, R. et al. (2016).
        <q>Successful Implementation of a Multicountry Clinical Surveillance
        and Data Collection System for Ebola Virus Disease in West Africa:
        Findings and Lessons Learned.</q> <em>Global Health: Science and
        Practice</em>, 4(3), 394<ndash />409.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 2: the STACK question that exemplifies this              -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-stack-crop-example">
    <title>The Same Mistake, Closer to Home</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The scale is nowhere near comparable, but the underlying mistake in
        the Ebola data <mdash /> deciding, without quite deciding, what an
        absence in the data actually means <mdash /> shows up constantly at
        far smaller scale. It shows up in this course's own assessment
        material.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-stack-crop-task">
      <title>Grown, sold, and profitable <mdash /> or not</title>

      <p>
        One question in this course's question bank gives students data on
        crops: whether each crop was grown, whether it was sold, and the
        profit recorded. The task is simple to state: which crop was most
        profitable, and which was least?
      </p>

      <p>
        A student's real, documented attempt at this question filtered the
        data to rows where the crop was sold, then calculated the average
        profit for each crop from that filtered set. By that approach,
        sorghum came out most profitable and millet least. The answer was
        marked wrong, and the student could not see why <mdash /> the
        arithmetic, on the data they had kept, was entirely correct.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-stack-crop-resolution">
      <title>What the filter quietly threw away</title>

      <p>
        Filtering to <q>sold</q> discarded every case where a crop was grown
        but <em>not</em> sold <mdash /> and threw those cases away in
        exactly the same manner as crops that were never grown at all, even
        though the two situations mean very different things. A crop that
        was grown and went unsold is a real economic outcome: money and
        effort spent, no return. A crop that was never grown is simply not
        part of the question.
      </p>

      <p>
        Treating a grown-but-unsold crop as a profit of <term>zero</term>,
        rather than discarding it, and excluding only the crops that were
        never grown, reverses the ranking completely: millet becomes the
        most profitable crop, and sorghum the least.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-stack-crop-lesson">
        <title>The filter was the analysis</title>
        <p>
          Nothing about the mean calculation itself was wrong, in either
          version. The entire result was decided at the filtering step,
          before any arithmetic happened, by a choice about what an
          <q>absent</q> value meant that was never made deliberately. This
          is the exact shape of the Ebola failures above, compressed into
          one line of code: <c>filter(sold == "yes")</c> looks like tidying
          the data up, and is actually a substantive claim about what the
          missing sales represent. Cleaning and recoding decisions are not
          preparation <em>before</em> the analysis. They routinely
          <em>are</em> the analysis, whether or not you notice yourself
          making them.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <!--
      STACK QUESTION: tracker ID 76, "missing values 1" (Moodle question
      bank entryid=108891, course 248). Reviewed and ready; narrated above
