      matching the pattern already used in ch1_intro.ptx, and this narrated
      version can be trimmed back to a short lead-in.
    -->
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 3: it happened again, elsewhere                          -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-phe-disaster">
    <title>Aside: The Same Failure Mode, on Another Continent</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Neither the scale nor the setting of the Ebola story is what
        matters most here. Six years later, with vastly more resources
        available, a strikingly similar failure hit another outbreak
        response <mdash /> not in West Africa, but in England, during
        COVID-19.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-phe-pipeline">
      <title>One pipeline, one file format</title>

      <p>
        Positive COVID-19 test results from testing labs across England were
        sent out as CSV files. Those files were imported into a template
        built on the old Excel <c>.xls</c> format <mdash /> the format Excel
        used before 2007 <mdash /> and forwarded on to contact tracers, whose
        job was to find and notify everyone each infected person had been in
        contact with.
      </p>

      <p>
        Nobody chose to lose data on purpose. The pipeline had been working,
        file after file, for months.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-phe-ceiling">
      <title>The ceiling nobody was watching for</title>

      <p>
        The old <c>.xls</c> format has a hard limit of 65,536 rows per sheet.
        Because each test result occupied several rows once the template's
        columns were filled in, a single file could hold only around 1,400
        test records before it was full.
      </p>

      <p>
        When a file hit that ceiling, Excel did not raise an error. It did
        not warn anyone that data was being left out. It simply stopped
        accepting new rows. Every test result beyond row 65,536 was silently
        dropped from the file that reached contact tracers, as if it had
        never been reported at all.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-phe-cost">
      <title>What it cost</title>

      <p>
        Between 25 September and 2 October 2020, 15,841 positive test results
        were lost this way. The people behind those results were not
        notified as positive cases in time, and <mdash /> just as seriously
        <mdash /> the people they had been in contact with were not traced
        and told to isolate, during a period when the outbreak was actively
        growing.
      </p>

      <p>
        The technical fault was fixed within days, by splitting the incoming
        files into smaller batches that stayed under the row limit. The
        health secretary at the time, Matt Hancock, said the underlying cause
        was a legacy system that a decision had already been made in July to
        replace. The system that failed in September was one everyone
        already knew needed retiring. It just had not been retired yet.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-phe-lesson">
        <title>The failure was silence, not error</title>
        <p>
          A crash would have been noticed immediately. A file that simply
          stops growing, with no warning and no error message, looks exactly
          like a file that succeeded. Just as with the Ebola case data,
          nothing about the output told anyone that anything had gone
          wrong. Checking that the number of rows you exported matches the
          number of rows you expected is one of the cheapest habits in data
          cleaning, and this is exactly what it exists to catch.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> UK Government, <q>Slides to accompany
        coronavirus press conference, CMO, 31 October 2020;</q>
        <url href="https://www.theregister.com/2020/10/05/excel_england_coronavirus_contact_error/" visual="theregister.com, 5 October 2020" />.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 4: the recoding disaster                                 -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-gene-names">
    <title>Aside: Recoding You Didn't Ask For</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Ebola and PHE are both about data disappearing. This one is about
        data quietly turning into something else <mdash /> and it did not
        happen once. It happened, unnoticed, across an entire scientific
        field, for more than a decade.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-genes-literal-software">
      <title>A very literal-minded piece of software</title>

      <p>
        Human genes have short alphanumeric names: <c>SEPT2</c>,
        <c>MARCH1</c>, <c>DEC1</c>. Genomics researchers routinely list
        hundreds of them in spreadsheets as part of their published research.
      </p>

      <p>
        Microsoft Excel, with its default settings, tries to be helpful. If a
        cell's contents look like a date <mdash /> and <c>SEPT2</c> looks
        enough like <q>2 September</q> to qualify <mdash /> Excel silently
        converts it, storing <c>2-Sep</c> in the cell instead of the gene name
        that was typed. <c>MARCH1</c> becomes <c>1-Mar</c>. No warning is
        shown. The gene name is simply gone, replaced by a date that has
        nothing to do with the data.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-genes-scale">
      <title>How widespread</title>

      <p>
        In 2016, researchers Mark Ziemann, Yotam Eren and Assam El-Osta
        screened the supplementary data files of 3,597 papers published in
        18 leading genomics journals between 2005 and 2015. Roughly
        <term>one in five</term> of the papers that included a
        supplementary Excel gene list had at least one gene name silently
        corrupted into a date or a number this way.
      </p>

      <p>
        These were not obscure, unread papers. They were published,
        peer-reviewed research, and the corrupted supplementary files were
        exactly the files other researchers would download and build on.
        Nobody had decided to recode <c>SEPT2</c> into a date. The software
        had done it for them, and it had gone unnoticed for years at a time,
        across an entire discipline.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-genes-fix">
      <title>The fix nobody wanted to need</title>

      <p>
        By 2020 the problem was serious enough that the body responsible for
        official human gene names, the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee
        (HGNC), renamed twenty-seven genes specifically to stop this from
        happening. <c>MARCH1</c> became <c>MARCHF1</c>. <c>SEPT1</c> became
        <c>SEPTIN1</c>. The genes themselves had not changed. Their names
        were changed because it was easier to rename twenty-seven genes than
        to get every spreadsheet in the world to stop guessing.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-genes-lesson">
        <title>Recoding is not always something you choose to do</title>
        <p>
          Every time you import a spreadsheet, a piece of software has
          already made decisions about how to interpret every cell <mdash />
          what looks like a number, what looks like a date, what looks like
          text <mdash /> before you have looked at any of it yourself.
          Cleaning data is not just fixing values that are obviously wrong.
          It includes checking that nothing was silently <q>fixed</q> for
          you, incorrectly, before you ever opened the file.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Ziemann, M., Eren, Y. and El-Osta, A. (2016).
        <q>Gene name errors are widespread in the scientific literature.</q>
        <em>Genome Biology</em>, 17, 177;
        <url href="https://www.genenames.org" visual="genenames.org" />
        (HGNC, 2020 renaming guidelines).
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 5: applied to the flamingo data                          -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-cleaning-applied">
    <title>Cleaning and Recoding, Applied</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> you loaded in
        <xref ref="ch-exploring-data" /> was already tidy: consistent lake
        names, consistent species labels, a sensible column for each
        variable. It did not start that way. Every dataset that looks that
        clean has already had this chapter done to it, by someone, before you
        ever saw it. This section does that work.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-bad-data-taxonomy">
      <title>Naming what you're looking for</title>

      <p>
        <q>Bad data</q> is not one problem. It is a handful of distinct
        problems that happen to all get called the same name, and each one
        needs a different fix. This chapter deals with three of them
        directly.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Inconsistent values</term> <mdash /> the same real-world
            thing, recorded in more than one way, so that R (or anyone
            counting by hand) treats it as several different things. This is
            the lake-name problem below, and it is exactly what
            <xref ref="sec-gene-names" /> shows going wrong at the scale of
            an entire research field, when the software does the
            <q>recoding</q> for you without telling you.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Duplicate records</term> <mdash /> the same case, entered
            more than once, usually because two systems or two people
            recorded it independently and nothing forced the records back
            together. <xref ref="ss-ebola-two-systems" /> is this problem at
            the scale of a national epidemic response.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Implausible values</term> <mdash /> an entry that is
            plausible-<em>looking</em> but cannot actually be true: a decimal
            point in the wrong place, a unit mixed up, a typo that still
            produces a valid number. These are the most dangerous errors of
            all, precisely because nothing about them looks wrong until you
            check.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Two more categories that get lumped in under <q>bad data</q> are
        deliberately not covered here. <term>Missing values</term> get a
        chapter of their own, <term>Handling Missing Values</term>, because
        the interesting question there is not how to spot a gap but what to
        do about it once you have. <term>Outliers</term> <mdash /> genuine,
        correctly recorded extreme values <mdash /> are a different problem
        again, and are covered properly once you have the statistical tools
        to say <em>how</em> extreme is extreme, in
        <term>Measuring and Observing Variability</term>. Confusing an
        outlier with an implausible value is itself a mistake worth avoiding:
        one is real and needs to be understood, the other is wrong and needs
        to be fixed.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-clean-inconsistent-labels">
      <title>Inconsistent categories</title>

      <p>
        Field data is transcribed by different people, on different days,
        sometimes typed in a hurry. A more realistic first look at the raw
        <c>lake</c> column, before anyone tidied it, might look like this.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        raw_lake &lt;- c("Bogoria", "bogoria", "BOGORIA", "Bogoria ",
                      "Nakuru", "Nakuru", "Elementaita", "elementaita")
        table(raw_lake)

        #&gt; raw_lake
        #&gt;     Bogoria    bogoria    BOGORIA   Bogoria  Elementaita elementaita
        #&gt;           1          1          1          1           1           1
        #&gt;      Nakuru
        #&gt;           2
      </program>

      <p>
        To R, <c>"Bogoria"</c>, <c>"bogoria"</c>, <c>"BOGORIA"</c> and
        <c>"Bogoria "</c> (with a trailing space) are four different values,
        not one. Any count or grouping done on this column before cleaning it
        would quietly split Bogoria's birds into four separate categories.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        clean_lake &lt;- trimws(raw_lake)          # remove leading/trailing spaces
        clean_lake &lt;- tools::toTitleCase(tolower(clean_lake))  # consistent case
        table(clean_lake)

        #&gt; clean_lake
        #&gt;     Bogoria Elementaita      Nakuru
        #&gt;           4           2           2
      </program>

      <p>
        Two functions, applied before anything else touches the column, and
        the four accidental categories collapse back into one real one. This
        is unglamorous work, and it is also exactly the kind of check the
        gene-name story shows the cost of skipping.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-clean-duplicates">
      <title>Duplicate records</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ss-ebola-two-systems" /> described the same case being
        entered twice, once from the laboratory result and once from the
        case investigation form, with nothing forcing the two back together.
        The R-scale version of that problem is just as easy to miss.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        bird_id &lt;- c(101, 102, 103, 103, 104)
        duplicated(bird_id)
        #&gt; [1] FALSE FALSE FALSE  TRUE FALSE

        sum(duplicated(bird_id))
        #&gt; [1] 1
      </program>

      <p>
        <c>duplicated()</c> flags every occurrence of a value <em>after</em>
        its first appearance, which is why counting <c>TRUE</c>s tells you
        how many extra rows exist, not how many birds are involved. A single
        duplicated ID is easy to spot in a vector of five. In a dataset of
        344 birds it is not, which is exactly why checking for duplicate IDs
        belongs in a routine cleaning pass rather than something you do only
        when a total looks suspicious.
      </p>

      <p>
        Notice what <c>duplicated()</c> cannot tell you: <em>why</em> the
        record was duplicated, or which of the two copies, if they disagree
        with each other, is correct. That judgement call is the same one
        Sierra Leone's response teams faced at national scale <mdash /> a
        duplicate is a signal to go and look, not a problem <c>duplicated()</c>
        solves for you on its own.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-clean-implausible">
      <title>Implausible values</title>

      <p>
        The lake-name problem was visible the moment you tabulated the
        column. This one is not: a value that is perfectly well-formed,
        sits quietly among correct ones, and is still wrong.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        bill &lt;- c(101.2, 96.1, 1012, 98.4, 94.1)
        summary(bill)

        #&gt;    Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max.
        #&gt;    94.1    96.1    98.4   280.4   101.2  1012.0
      </program>

      <p>
        No lesser flamingo has a 1,012 mm bill; the real bird measured
        101.2 mm, and a decimal point was lost somewhere between the field
        sheet and the spreadsheet. Unlike the lake-name problem,
        <c>table()</c> is no help here <mdash /> every value is numeric and
        individually plausible, and nothing about <c>1012</c> looks like an
        error in isolation. What gives it away is knowing something about
        flamingos: bill lengths in this dataset simply do not reach four
        figures. A basic sanity-range check catches exactly this.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        which(bill &lt; 50 | bill &gt; 200)
        #&gt; [1] 3
      </program>

      <p>
        A range check like this only works because someone supplied the
        range <mdash /> R has no idea, on its own, what a plausible flamingo
        bill length is. This is the one category of bad data in this chapter
        that genuinely cannot be caught by a general-purpose function; it
        requires knowing something about the thing being measured.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-clean-checking-what-doesnt-fit">
      <title>Checking systematically, not just when something looks wrong</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-exploring-data" /> discovered the missing masses by
        accident, because <c>mean()</c> returned <c>NA</c>. A proper cleaning
        pass checks every column, deliberately, rather than waiting to be
        surprised.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        colSums(is.na(flamingos))

        #&gt;           lake        species bill_length_mm  bill_depth_mm  leg_length_mm
        #&gt;              0              0              0              4              0
        #&gt;    body_mass_g            sex           year
        #&gt;              8              7              0
      </program>

      <p>
        Four missing bill depths, eight missing masses, seven missing sexes.
        Finding the gaps is only half the job. The more useful question is
        <em>why</em> those three variables specifically, and not
        <c>lake</c> or <c>year</c>, are the ones with gaps <mdash /> the same
        question, at vastly smaller stakes, that Owada et al. were asking
        about incomplete case investigation forms. In fieldwork, determining
        sex often requires closer handling or genetic testing that a
        stressed or struggling bird may not get; a scale battery can fail
        mid-morning and every mass after that point goes unrecorded for the
        rest of the day. Reasoning like this, before deciding what to do
        about the gaps, is what turns <q>the data has missing values</q> into
        something you can actually act on <mdash /> and it is the question
        <xref ref="ch-exploring-data" /> deliberately left open, and that
        <term>Handling Missing Values</term> will return to formally.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-recoding-variables">
      <title>Recoding variables</title>

      <p>
        Cleaning fixes values that are wrong. Recoding changes values that
        are already correct into a form better suited to the question being
        asked. The <c>sex</c> column is a realistic case: different people
        entering data by hand might use <c>"M"</c>, <c>"m"</c>,
        <c>"male"</c> or <c>"Male"</c> for the same thing.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        raw_sex &lt;- c("M", "male", "Male", "F", "female", "f")

        clean_sex &lt;- factor(
          ifelse(tolower(substr(raw_sex, 1, 1)) == "m", "male", "female")
        )
        table(clean_sex)

        #&gt; clean_sex
        #&gt; female   male
        #&gt;      3      3
      </program>

      <p>
        A second, common kind of recoding creates a new categorical variable
        out of a numeric one, so it can be used for grouping or plotting. R's
        factors carry a fixed, named set of <term>levels</term>, and renaming
        them is a recode in its own right.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        flamingos$species_short &lt;- factor(
          flamingos$species,
          levels = c("Greater Flamingo", "Lesser Flamingo"),
          labels = c("Greater", "Lesser")
        )
        levels(flamingos$species_short)
        #&gt; [1] "Greater" "Lesser"
      </program>

      <p>
        Nothing about the underlying birds has changed. What has changed is
        how easy the variable is to read, tabulate, and plot <mdash /> which
        is the entire point of recoding, and exactly the point that went
        wrong, silently, in the gene-name story above. It is also exactly
        the choice <mdash /> what to do with an absence, or a category that
        does not quite fit <mdash /> that decided the outcome in
        <xref ref="ss-stack-crop-resolution" />.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  OBJECTIVES                                                       -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <objectives xml:id="objectives-cleaning-recoding">
    <title>By the end of this topic you will be able to</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Understand why data cleaning matters, including from real cases where skipping it caused measurable harm.</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify and handle common data quality issues: inconsistent categories, duplicate records, implausible values, and unexplained gaps.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recode variables deliberately, and recognise when software may have already recoded something for you without asking.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognise a filtering or recoding decision as a substantive analytical choice, not neutral tidying, particularly around what an absent value is taken to mean.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-cleaning-recoding">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-crop-recap">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In your own words, explain why filtering the crop data to
          <c>sold == "yes"</c> before averaging (<xref ref="ss-stack-crop-task" />)
          produced a wrong answer even though the averaging itself was done
          correctly. Then describe one other variable, in any dataset you
          have worked with, where <q>missing</q> and <q>zero</q> would mean
          genuinely different things, and explain what filtering it out
          would silently assume.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-ebola-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-ebola-two-systems" /> describes case data being entered
          independently in two places, laboratory records and case
          investigation forms, with no process forcing the two back
          together. Suggest one concrete check, applied at the point of data
          entry rather than afterwards, that could have caught a duplicate
          case before it reached a national situation report.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-phe-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-phe-cost" /> notes that the PHE failure was dangerous
          specifically because it was silent: the file that lost data looked
          exactly like a file that hadn't. Suggest one concrete check that,
          if run automatically every time a file was produced, would have
          caught this problem within a day rather than a week.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-lake-labels">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the <c>raw_lake</c> vector from <xref ref="ss-clean-inconsistent-labels" />
          as a starting point, write R code that produces a clean factor
          with exactly three levels, without using <c>tools::toTitleCase()</c>.
          There is more than one correct approach.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-duplicate-ids">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the <c>bird_id</c> vector from <xref ref="ss-clean-duplicates" />,
          write R code that returns the actual duplicated <em>values</em>
          (not just <c>TRUE</c>/<c>FALSE</c> flags). Then explain, in your
          own words, why a low count from <c>sum(duplicated(x))</c> is
          reassuring but not sufficient on its own <mdash /> what would you
          still need to check before trusting that the data has no
          duplicate-record problem?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-implausible-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-clean-implausible" /> catches an implausible bill
          length using a hard-coded range, <c>bill &lt; 50 | bill &gt; 200</c>.
          Suggest a plausible range check for <c>leg_length_mm</c> and for
          <c>body_mass_g</c>, using whatever you can infer from the numbers
          already given elsewhere in this module. What is the risk of
          setting a range check too narrow, as opposed to too wide?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-recode-mass-class">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Recode <c>body_mass_g</c> into a new categorical variable
          <c>size_class</c> with two levels, <q>light</q> and <q>heavy</q>,
          split at 2500 g. Then cross-tabulate <c>size_class</c> against
          <c>species</c>. Does the split you chose separate the two species
          cleanly, or does it cut through one of them? What does that tell
          you about choosing a threshold for a recode versus choosing one for
          a plot?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</chapter>
